<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dirk III, Count of Holland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 981–1039</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Dirk III of Holland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dirk III, Count of Holland, ruled the West Frisian county that later became Holland from 993. He seized the marshy lands around the Merwede and levied tolls on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> shipping, and in 1018 his forces defeated an imperial army sent against him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vlaardingen">Battle of Vlaardingen</a>. The victory secured his gains and laid foundations for the county's later power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dirk III, also called Dirik or Theodoric, was count of the area that became Holland, then often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-frisia">West Frisia</a>, from 993 to 27 May 1039. The name Holland was first used for it only in 1101. Most of the territory was boggy and subject to constant flooding and so sparsely populated, with settlement mainly in the coastal dunes and on raised ground near the rivers.</p><p>Dirk belonged to the house of Holland. His mother, Luitgard of Luxemburg, was regent from 993 to 1005, while he was a minor. She was the sister-in-law of Emperor Henry II, and with his help she kept the county for her son. She went on to use her family connections to win imperial help for Dirk, and an imperial army once helped him suppress a Frisian revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Before 1018 Dirk was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Henry II, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, Utrecht and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> all contested ownership of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, which lay in a strategic position. Traders from Utrecht, the largest trading town of the German kings in the area, had to pass through his lands by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Vecht to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, and another route ran from Tiel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>Along that route Dirk built a stronghold at Vlaardingen, in a newly habitable area where many Frisians had settled at his invitation. He was not permitted to levy tolls or hinder trade, but he defied imperial rule and, with the Frisians there, stopped passing ships and demanded tolls. Merchants of Tiel complained to the king and to Bishop Adelbold of Utrecht of violence by his men. Henry decided to end Dirk's rule and awarded his lands to Adelbold.</p><p>A large imperial army, supplied by the region's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and commanded by Godfrey II, Duke of Lower Lorraine, marched on Vlaardingen. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vlaardingen">Battle of Vlaardingen</a> in 1018 it was destroyed. Many imperial commanders died and Godfrey was captured. Dirk kept his lands, went on levying tolls and later gained more land to the east at the expense of the Bishop of Utrecht. After Henry's death in 1024 he supported Conrad II for the kingship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dirk is thought to have gone on pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> around 1030, hence his nickname Hierosolymita, the Jerusalemite. He died on 27 May 1039 and was buried at Egmond Abbey. He had married Othelindis, perhaps a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-i-duke-of-saxony">Bernard I, Duke of Saxony</a>, and was succeeded by his sons Dirk IV and then Floris I. After his death imperial armies tried several times to reclaim the lands of the Frisian counts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-i-count-of-flanders">Robert I, Count of Flanders</a>, helped Dirk V, Dirk III's grandson, to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> to the counts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dirk III of Holland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dirk III</b> defeated an imperial army at Vlaardingen in 1018; William II was elected King of the Romans in 1247 and began the Binnenhof at The Hague.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Egmond aan den Hoef, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.62,4.65&amp;year=1018" aria-label="Egmond aan den Hoef on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4654 349 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Dirk III, Count of Holland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5104 619h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5104 619h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.62,4.65&amp;year=1018">What was here in 1018? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
